Municipal Financial Management System (MFMS)
Project A — Technical Report | PAP521S: Programming in Practice

Section 1: Introduction
The Municipal Financial Management System (MFMS) is a menu-driven C application developed by a team of seven students for PAP521S Programming in Practice. The system manages employee records, departmental budgets, suppliers, and municipal assets, and produces summary reports.

Section 2: Problem Description
Municipalities handle large amounts of financial and personnel data. Manual record-keeping is slow, error-prone, and hard to search. A software solution is needed to organize this information efficiently.

Section 3: System Objectives
- Provide a clear menu-driven interface
- Allow adding, displaying, and searching employee records
- Track departmental budgets and expenditure
- Manage supplier information
- Maintain an asset register
- Generate summary reports

Section 4: System Features
- Employee Management: add, display, search
- Budget Management: allocate budgets, record expenditure, flag over-budget departments
- Supplier Management: add, display, search by name
- Asset Management: add, display, search by ID
- Reports: employee, budget, supplier, and asset summaries

Section 5: Program Design
The system uses a modular design. main.c provides the menu and calls each module's function. Each module has its own .c and .h files. Data is stored using parallel arrays. Concepts used: arrays, loops, if/else, switch, functions, strings, input/output.

Section 6: Challenges Encountered
- Handling input with scanf in C
- Coordinating Git branches across 7 developers
- Resolving a merge conflict caused by an accidental edit to main.c
- Working around campus network restrictions (SSH port 22 blocked)

Section 7: Solutions Implemented
- Used feature branches and pull requests for every change
- Protected the main branch to require reviews
- Restored main.c from Git history when it was accidentally modified
- Configured SSH to use port 443 as a workaround

Section 8: Conclusion
The MFMS successfully manages municipal employee, budget, supplier, and asset data through an integrated menu system. The project demonstrates the effective use of C programming concepts and teamwork with Git and GitHub. It provides a solid foundation for Project B extensions.
